TIProjectTemplate.displayName.Project_CataTweaks_CouncilSize7=Deep Cover Handlers
TIProjectTemplate.summary.Project_CataTweaks_CouncilSize7=A seventh seat, paid for out of the budget that ran an agent in someone else's council.
TIProjectTemplate.description.Project_CataTweaks_CouncilSize7=Running an asset inside a rival council was never cheap. A handler, a cut-out, a courier route that has to survive its own success, and a case officer whose entire working life is one person who might be lying. Covert Operations taught us to sustain two of them at once. This teaches us what the ledger has been saying all along: that the same apparatus, turned inward and given a name badge, seats another of our own people at our own table. We are not recruiting a spy. We are spending the spy on ourselves.
TIProjectTemplate.displayName.Project_CataTweaks_CouncilSize8=Shadow Cabinet
TIProjectTemplate.summary.Project_CataTweaks_CouncilSize8=An eighth seat, and the last of the handlers comes in from the cold.
TIProjectTemplate.description.Project_CataTweaks_CouncilSize8=Every council keeps a second council: the people who would take over if the first one were arrested, discredited, or vaporized. Ours has been meeting in safe houses and paying for itself out of the intelligence line, which is a tidy arrangement until the day it is needed and nobody can find it. So we seat it. The last handler surrenders their network, the last cut-out is stood down, and the chair that funded a listening post in a rival's cabinet is carried upstairs and set at the head of the table. Nothing is now hidden from us that we did not choose to stop watching.
TIProjectTemplate.displayName.Project_TheSunNeverSets=The Sun Never Sets
TIProjectTemplate.summary.Project_TheSunNeverSets=We reassert the claims of the whole Empire, not merely the settler dominions.
TIProjectTemplate.description.Project_TheSunNeverSets=Commonwealth Restored brought back the easy half: the dominions that never really left, whose flags still carry ours in the corner. The rest of the map was always the point. Delhi and Dhaka, Lagos and Nairobi, the Cape and the Straits, the sugar islands, and the thirteen colonies that got away - all of it administered once from a single square mile of London. We are not asking for it back. We are filing the paperwork that says it never stopped being ours, and letting the rest follow.
TIProjectTemplate.displayName.Project_MareNostrum=Mare Nostrum
TIProjectTemplate.summary.Project_MareNostrum=The Republic reasserts its authority over the Mediterranean world.
TIProjectTemplate.description.Project_MareNostrum=Every road still runs to this city, whatever the milestones have been repainted to say. The sea between the provinces is not a border and never was; it is the courtyard of a single house whose tenants have grown forgetful. Hispania, Gaul, Africa, Achaea, Asia, Aegyptus, Syria - the names survive on our maps because nobody has ever invented better ones. We begin with the sea, because the sea is ours.
TIProjectTemplate.displayName.Project_ImperiumSineFine=Imperium Sine Fine
TIProjectTemplate.summary.Project_ImperiumSineFine=Britannia, the eastern roads, Aksum - and the far shore of the Ocean.
TIProjectTemplate.description.Project_ImperiumSineFine=Virgil promised an empire without end, and the surveyors took him literally. Britannia at the cold edge, Armenia and Mesopotamia where the legions marched and mostly regretted it, Nubia up the river, and Aksum at the far end of the Red Sea run, where our coins turn up in the dirt because our merchants got there first. And then the Ocean, which the ancients took for the edge of the world and which turns out to be merely wide. There is land on the other side of it, currently administered by people who arrived there fifteen centuries after we stopped looking. An empire without end does not recognise an ocean as an end. It recognises an unsurveyed province.
TIProjectTemplate.displayName.Project_NewLiberia=New Liberia
TIProjectTemplate.summary.Project_NewLiberia=Monrovia, Freetown and Libreville: one coast, one founding idea.
TIProjectTemplate.description.Project_NewLiberia=Three powers had the same notion within forty years of each other, and none of them thought to compare notes. The Americans put freed slaves ashore at Monrovia, the British at Freetown, the French at Libreville - the free town, in case the point was missed. Between them lies the Kru coast, whose sailors crewed the ships that carried all three. The descendants of those settlements have more in common with each other than with the empires that dropped them here, and they have started saying so.
